title ALES67 — Lotus's echo in real time, a delay named in milliseconds
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first, take the next agent-doable lap, send each finished increment, stop at custody gates
think ALES66 opened the time-based wing but named its delay in sample indices; the family's established pattern gives each index-named rung a real-time twin (fade_ms over the fade, place_at, marker_time over the markers). The echo wants the same.
think Chose to build it as its own module echo_time.rye (ALES67), mirroring marker_time.rye being a separate module rather than editing ALES5 or ALES66 — additive, touches nothing proven.
think echo_ms converts start_ms/count_ms/delay_ms through clock.samples_for and delegates to echo unchanged; the grounding law is that the clock adds only the units, so the ms-named echo equals the index-named echo byte-for-byte.
think Nice property fell out: samples_for is divTrunc(rate*ms,1000), so a sub-sample delay (0 ms) converts to 0 and the echo already refuses BadDelay — a sub-sample echo refuses honestly rather than silently doing nothing.
obs First selftest RED: case 2 used count_ms 200 at 48 kHz, converting to 9600 samples, past max_clip (4096, = 4*wire.max_samples=4*1024) — an uncaught DurationTooLong. Fixed by sizing the clip to 240 samples (5 ms at 48 kHz) so count_ms 5 covers it exactly. Second run GREEN.
obs Confirmed the real-rate arithmetic on metal: at 48 kHz, 1 ms = 48 samples, the first echo sitting exactly at index 48 at half the impulse.
loom witness=ales_echo_time_witness cases=6 green=1 reruns=ales66_echo max_clip=4096
